  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe warming trend continues as the center of a growing high pressure \nsystem moves over Arizona today. The system will peak in intensity \nand be centered near the southern Colorado River Valley by Saturday. \nThis is when we can expect the hottest temperatures with highs well \ninto the 90s for valleys and mountains of Orange and San Diego \nCounties and over 115 degrees across the lower deserts. The high \nweakens slightly on Sunday, but temperatures will still remain hot \naway from the immediate coast. \n\nWith the hot temperatures, please severely limit or postpone outdoor \nrecreation if you are in areas away from the coast, since \ntemperatures will be well into the 70s and 80s by the mid-morning \nhours. For those without A/C, make sure to go to air conditioned \nareas and drink plenty of water. Warm and humid nights in the \n60s/70s/80s will lead to less relief from the heat as well.\n\nBy early next week, the high weakens subtly. The position of where \nthe high will go is still somewhat in flux. This will bring a \ndegree or two of cooling for inland areas each day, but warm \ntemperatures will still remain. Temperatures will stay near to \nslightly above normal by Monday onward next week. The high may go\ninto a position that will bring in a subtle return of monsoonal \nmoisture and greater humidity to the region, but rain chances \nstill seem slim near 15% or less across our mountain areas by \nWednesday into the latter half of next week.",
